The code statements that lead to the bug are:
gva_t gva = kvm_rax_read(vcpu);
u32 asid = kvm_rcx_read(vcpu);
kvm_mmu_invlpg(vcpu, gva);
Reason:
These statements are executed unconditionally without first verifying that EFER.SVME is set. According to the AMD architecture, INVLPGA must cause a #UD when SVM is disabled (EFER.SVME=0). The missing permission check allows the instruction to proceed even when SVM is not enabled.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (nested_svm_check_permissions(vcpu))
	return 1;
Reason:
This added check calls `nested_svm_check_permissions(vcpu)`, which returns non-zero if EFER.SVME is not set (or other SVM permission checks fail). In that case, the handler returns 1, causing KVM to inject a #UD into the guest, thereby enforcing the architectural requirement that INVLPGA faults when SVM is disabled.

